From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] shaders/colorspace: the dynamic tone mapping target only
 where it is asked for

Where the source has a scene average (HDR10+, CIE Y metadata such as
Dolby Vision level 1, or a measured one) and is brighter than the
display, libplacebo tone maps into a virtual target between the peak of
the display and that of the source, and lowers the whole picture by the
ratio of the two: some average brightness traded for headroom for the
highlights. What the display could show as mastered then comes out
darker by that ratio: 0.89 to 0.98 for the pictures of a Dolby Vision
test stream mapped to SDR with their level 1.

A field is added, pl_color_map_params.dynamic_target, false by default,
unlike upstream: the target is then the peak of the display, as for a
source without a scene average. It is at the end of the structure, and
an option (dynamic_target) as well.

Measured with Kodi's Dolby Vision bridge on Mesa's software OpenGL ES
(zink on lavapipe), against the reconstruction of each picture in double
precision, in Delta E ITP (ITU-R BT.2124): 14 pictures of a profile 7
FEL stream, with and without the residual, and 3 of profile 5, mapped to
SDR (BT.709, 100 cd/m^2, HDR reference white at the display's white) and
to a display of 150 cd/m^2 in DCI-P3 (BT.2390, the perceptual gamut
mapping), 45 mappings with pixels the display can show and 48 with
highlights:
- what the display can show: the median lower in 43 (the same in the
  other two, the darkest picture), the 99th percentile lower in all 45;
  the largest gains of the median from 7.60 to 1.35 (SDR) and from 17.90
  to 13.63 (the display of 150 cd/m^2), and between 5 and 30 cd/m^2 from
  5.57 to 0.07;
- the highlights, above the peak of the display, which no mapping shows
  as mastered: within 1 Delta E ITP of what the dynamic target gives in
  every picture (the median higher in 33, lower in 13, by at most 0.73;
  the 99th percentile by at most 0.95), their light at their 50th to
  99.9th percentiles within 1.1 cd/m^2, and its spread in PQ from their
  10th to their 99th percentile at most 0.0013 narrower;
- HDR10 and HLG pictures, which have no scene average: the same bytes.

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, that of libplacebo and of the files changed,
whose notices stay as they are.
---
 src/include/libplacebo/shaders/colorspace.h | 10 ++++++++++
 src/options.c                               |  1 +
 src/shaders/colorspace.c                    |  4 +++-
 3 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/include/libplacebo/shaders/colorspace.h b/src/include/libplacebo/shaders/colorspace.h
index 0e8cbb9..44095ad 100644
--- a/src/include/libplacebo/shaders/colorspace.h
+++ b/src/include/libplacebo/shaders/colorspace.h
@@ -390,6 +390,16 @@ struct pl_color_map_params {
     PL_DEPRECATED_IN(v6.269) enum pl_rendering_intent intent; // see `gamut_mapping`
     PL_DEPRECATED_IN(v6.269) enum pl_gamut_mode gamut_mode;   // see `gamut_mapping`
     PL_DEPRECATED_IN(v6.290) float hybrid_mix;                // removed
+
+    // Hades: tone map into a dynamic target, a virtual peak between the
+    // display's and the source's chosen from the scene average of the
+    // source's dynamic metadata (HDR10+, CIE Y such as Dolby Vision level 1,
+    // or the measured one), and lower the whole picture by the ratio of the
+    // display's peak to it: some average brightness traded for headroom for
+    // the highlights. Off by default (false), unlike upstream: what the
+    // display can show is then shown as mastered, and only what it cannot
+    // is compressed into its peak.
+    bool dynamic_target;
 };
 
 #define PL_COLOR_MAP_DEFAULTS                                   \
diff --git a/src/options.c b/src/options.c
index 5fb798b..f3b2f81 100644
--- a/src/options.c
+++ b/src/options.c
@@ -1144,6 +1144,7 @@ const struct pl_opt_t pl_option_list[] = {
              {"cie_y",     PL_HDR_METADATA_CIE_Y})),
     OPT_FLOAT("mastering_clip", "Mastering peak soft clip strength", color_map_params.mastering_clip, .max = 1.0),
     OPT_FLOAT("film_strength", "Film response strength", color_map_params.film_strength, .max = 2.0),
+    OPT_BOOL("dynamic_target", "Dynamic tone mapping target", color_map_params.dynamic_target),
     OPT_INT("tone_lut_size", "Tone mapping LUT size", color_map_params.lut_size, .max = 4096),
     OPT_FLOAT("contrast_recovery", "HDR contrast recovery strength", color_map_params.contrast_recovery, .max = 2.0),
     OPT_FLOAT("contrast_smoothness", "HDR contrast recovery smoothness", color_map_params.contrast_smoothness, .min = 1.0, .max = 32.0),
diff --git a/src/shaders/colorspace.c b/src/shaders/colorspace.c
index 27956be..884058f 100644
--- a/src/shaders/colorspace.c
+++ b/src/shaders/colorspace.c
@@ -2128,7 +2128,9 @@ void pl_shader_color_map_ex(pl_shader sh, const struct pl_color_map_params *para
     // linearly: trading some average brightness buys headroom for the tone
     // mapping and the highlights, which gives far better results than
     // compressing e.g. a 10000 nits source directly into a 203 nits target.
-    if (obj && scene_avg > 0 && !params->inverse_tone_mapping &&
+    // Hades: only where the caller asks for it (dynamic_target).
+    if (obj && params->dynamic_target && scene_avg > 0 &&
+        !params->inverse_tone_mapping &&
         tone.input_max > tone.output_max + 1e-4f)
     {
         // Smooth maximum of the display peak and a fixed perceptual (PQ)
